    "submission": "submission-review-c3b.json",
    "summary": "Turn 87 reviewed the applied-state finding on its driven history, removing its prepared-record caveat.\n\nState on entry. The artifact f1d4a9f06e81464aa803ee7809e4e5c6 (executed as 3843884f…) reported violated with the oracle correspondence unreviewed. Its stream shows the history first — proposal_committed with leader_status 4 and third_status 4, i.e. the owner admitted a client proposal, ran the PreAccept round, fast-committed and the third replica installed the Commit — and then applied_state_divergence with peer and third values 04 against the recoverer's (void).\n\nReview judgement (checker_correspondence, no_issue_found). Requirement versus predicate: the claim is that replicas holding the same instance committed apply the same effect for its key, and the predicate asserts applied_match == true; the run reports false with the applied values printed alongside the commit that produced them, so the assertion is evaluated on the replicas' own stores. History and legality: the committed record is no longer installed — the harness admits a client proposal with the real handler, runs the PreAccept round, lets the owner fast-commit and broadcasts the Commit, which the third replica installs — while the recovering replica holds no record because the harness keeps it out of the proposal's traffic; the recovery is entered directly, which the claim's declared scope records. Oracle and identity: each effect is read after the real executor applies the replica's committed record, and the instance identity is shared with the prerequisite reply. Limits: execution goes through the executor entry rather than the production loop (so scheduling and watermarks are outside), one instance and one key are compared, the configuration is N=3 with Thrifty, and the recovery's timeout trigger is confirmed separately (C8) but not driven here; the earlier prepared-record artifact remains superseded for basis.\n\nFrontier. The applied-state finding now rests on the same driven history as the recovery finding, so the two headline consequences of the confirmed defect — divergent committed vectors and divergent applied effects — are both established from a legal proposal, commit and recovery sequence rather than from prepared records. Twelve claim families are settled, the map is current at version 17, two candidates remain paused with scoped resume conditions, one item remains in-exercisable in-package (the reader-side reaction to a displaced stream), and the attribution issue on the superseded C1 artifact stays visible as bookkeeping. No requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened."
